<title>How we halved DNS build delay and cut memory use by 85%</title>
<link href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/dns-targeted-zone-builds/"/>
<id>urn:rss-system-design:top3:b10d0651ae</id>
<updated>2026-11-02T12:00:00Z</updated>
<author><name>Cloudflare</name></author>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="networking-edge" label="Networking &amp; Edge"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="data-pipelines" label="Data Pipelines &amp; Streaming"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="storage-caching" label="Databases, Storage &amp; Caching"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="scalability" label="Scale &amp; Performance"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="consistency" label="Consistency &amp; Correctness"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="reliability" label="Reliability &amp; Resilience"/>
<summary>Shows the design decision to run only the smallest provably safe DNS build, with fresh-state verification, Kafka soft commits and mirror testing, which cut peak memory by 85% and halved median build completion delay.</summary>
</entry>
<entry>
<title>Learning new skills without forgetting: Self-Distillation vs GRPO</title>
<link href="https://careersatdoordash.com/blog/learning-new-skills-without-forgetting-self-distillation-vs-grpo/"/>
<id>urn:rss-system-design:top3:8f34d45bee</id>
<updated>2026-11-02T11:59:59Z</updated>
<author><name>DoorDash</name></author>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="ml-platform" label="ML Platform &amp; Models"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="evaluation" label="Testing &amp; Evaluation"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="cost" label="Cost Efficiency"/>
<summary>Shows the design decision between Self-Distillation and GRPO for continual post-training, weighing GRPO's higher compute against forgetting, as self-distilled Nemotron lost 30 points on logic while GRPO never dropped more than 0.2 points.</summary>
</entry>
<entry>
<title>Scaling Git at OpenAI</title>
<link href="https://openai.com/index/scaling-git-at-openai"/>
<id>urn:rss-system-design:top3:de4cf53f9d</id>
<updated>2026-11-02T11:59:58Z</updated>
<author><name>OpenAI</name></author>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="dev-platform" label="Developer Platform &amp; CI/CD"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:subject" term="storage-caching" label="Databases, Storage &amp; Caching"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="scalability" label="Scale &amp; Performance"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="consistency" label="Consistency &amp; Correctness"/>
<category scheme="urn:rss-system-design:concern" term="evolvability" label="Migrations &amp; Evolution"/>
<summary>Shows the design decision to relax repository-wide consistency to per-ref causal consistency and reuse packfiles across fetches, which reduced median push latency by nearly 80% and serves nearly a petabyte of Git data per day.</summary>
</entry>
